What:		/sys/kernel/mm/xswap/rswap_attach
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Linux memory management mailing list <linux-mm@kvack.org>
Description:	Attach or detach the rswap digest program of a VMA
		(only present with CONFIG_RSWAP=y).

		Input is a single line, written to the file:

		::

		    <virtual-address-hex> <program-fd>

		The virtual address is read in hexadecimal and must lie inside
		a VMA of the writing task; it is used as written, it is not
		rounded to a page boundary.  The second field is a decimal file
		descriptor of a loaded BPF program, or 0 to detach the program
		that is currently attached.

		The program is the digest source for the pages that VMA maps.
		rswap calls it from the swap-out path, while the page is still
		mapped, with a writable context:

		::

		    struct rswap_hash_ctx {
			__u64 vaddr;
			__u8  hash[48];
		    };

		The program must be of type BPF_PROG_TYPE_SYSCALL and fill
		``hash`` with the digest user space wants used as the remote
		key for the page at ``vaddr``, returning non-zero.  A zero
		return value, or no attached program, means "no digest"; rswap
		then falls back to the digest attached to the base page through
		/proc/sys/vm/rswap/attach if there is one, and fails writeout
		with -ENODATA if there is none either.  The digest is used as
		the key of one base page in the remote key-value store; the
		kernel never derives it from the swap entry or from the page.

		The program runs under the page table lock, in the middle of
		unmapping a folio.  It must not sleep, must not fault, must not
		access user memory, and must not take locks that reclaim or the
		page table lock holder may already hold.  It annotates a page
		for swap-out; it does not read, modify or write back anything.

		The kernel does not recompute or verify the digest against the
		page contents, so user space must make sure it still matches,
		and it must supply a new one after modifying a page.  A digest
		is a property of the mapping that attached the program: a VMA
		copy (fork()) does not inherit the program, and a range split by
		mprotect() or mremap() stops resolving digests.

		Writing requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN.  Attaching a program replaces a
		previously attached one; on error the previous program stays
		attached.  Errors: -EPERM without CAP_SYS_ADMIN, -EINVAL for a
		malformed line or for a program that is not a
		BPF_PROG_TYPE_SYSCALL program whose context fits
		rswap_hash_ctx, -ENOENT when no VMA covers the address, -ESRCH
		for a task without an mm, -EINTR when the mmap lock could not be
		taken, and the program-descriptor lookup's own errno for an
		invalid descriptor.
